WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.268
You type three letters into a search box and suggestions appear instantly. Under the hood, a trie walks those letters in three steps.

00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:12.397
Pause and think.

00:00:12.797 --> 00:00:16.187
How long does it take to check a prefix of length m in a trie?

00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:23.902
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.812
This lesson covers tries, the tree built for words and prefixes. It powers autocomplete.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:40.475
New word: trie. A prefix tree storing strings letter by letter. Picture it like this: A dictionary's thumb tabs: A → AP → APP.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.763
Think of a giant filing cabinet for words.

00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:51.798
The first drawer is chosen by the first letter, the folder inside by the second letter, and so on.

00:00:52.248 --> 00:00:57.762
Words that start the same way share the same drawers. Car and cat share the drawers c and a.

00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:01.904
A small flag on a folder says: a complete word ends here.

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:08.699
Here is a trie holding the words a, at, and to, drawn as a tree of letter codes.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:15.303
Searching for at means: from the root, take the a edge, then the t edge, then check the end flag.

00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:25.696
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:01:27.346 --> 00:01:32.176
If an edge is missing, the word is not there, and we stop immediately.

00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:37.211
The cost depends only on the length of the word, not on how many words are stored.

00:01:38.561 --> 00:01:39.781
Some vocabulary.

00:01:40.231 --> 00:01:48.137
A node usually stores up to twenty six child pointers, one per letter, or a small hash map when the alphabet is large.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:57.877
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:01:58.277 --> 00:02:06.636
New word: pointer. A variable holding the memory address of something else. Picture it like this: A note saying "the book is on shelf 4".

00:02:08.279 --> 00:02:13.945
Search asks whether a full word exists. StartsWith asks whether any word begins with a prefix.

00:02:14.395 --> 00:02:21.929
For autocomplete, each node can also cache its top suggestions, so typing a prefix returns results instantly.

00:02:22.078 --> 00:02:33.224
New word: cache. A small fast store of recently used data so you don't fetch it from the slow place again. Picture it like this: Keeping the salt next to the stove instead of in the basement.

00:02:35.728 --> 00:02:38.643
Word Search Two combines a trie with a grid search.

00:02:39.093 --> 00:02:45.014
Instead of searching for each word separately, we walk the grid once and follow trie edges as we go.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:51.420
The moment the trie says no word starts like this, we turn back. That pruning is the whole speed up.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.627
Interview tips for tries.

00:02:55.077 --> 00:03:00.174
Say why a hash set is not enough: it cannot answer prefix questions cheaply.

00:03:00.624 --> 00:03:07.218
Mention memory: twenty six pointers per node adds up, so compressed tries or sorted arrays are used in production.

00:03:07.378 --> 00:03:15.818
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:03:17.428 --> 00:03:22.723
And for systems: IP routing tables use a binary trie for longest prefix match.

00:03:22.728 --> 00:03:36.172
New word: I P. Internet Protocol address (a network address); also Intellectual Property — a licensed chip design block. Picture it like this: A street address for a computer (or a licensed blueprint for a chip part).

00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:44.494
Tries trade memory for fast prefix lookups. Practice Implement Trie, then Word Search Two.

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:45.710
Back to the question.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.500
How long does it take to check a prefix of length m in a trie?

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.174
O of m, no matter how many words are stored.

00:03:54.374 --> 00:03:55.245
Quick recap.

00:03:55.645 --> 00:03:57.154
Each edge is one letter.

00:03:57.554 --> 00:03:59.412
Shared prefixes share nodes.

00:03:59.812 --> 00:04:02.087
Great for autocomplete and word search.
